## 🧠 Column vs Measure (Critical Rule)

### ✅ Calculated Columns (SQL or Power BI)

| Field               | Why                    |
| ------------------- | ---------------------- |
| cohort_month        | Fixed per customer     |
| months_since_cohort | Per customer per month |

These **must be columns**, not measures.

---

### 📐 Measures (Power BI)

#### Cohort Size

```DAX
Cohort Size =
CALCULATE(
    DISTINCTCOUNT('vw_dim_customers'[customer_id])
)
```

Counts customers in the cohort row.

---

#### Retained Customers

```DAX
Retained Customers =
CALCULATE(
    DISTINCTCOUNT('vw_fact_customer_month_cohort'[customer_id])
)
```

Filtered naturally by:

* Cohort Month (row)
* Months Since Cohort (column)

No TREATAS required if relationships are correct.

---

#### Retention %

```DAX
Retention % =
DIVIDE([
    Retained Customers
], [Cohort Size])
```

---

## 🔗 Power BI Relationships

* `vw_dim_customers[customer_id]` → `vw_fact_customer_month_cohort[customer_id]`
* Calendar → `sale_month`

Single direction is sufficient.

---

## ❌ Common Mistakes (Avoid These)

* ❌ Calculating months since cohort as a measure
* ❌ Using item-level `fact_sales` for cohort math
* ❌ Heavy use of `TREATAS` to fix modeling issues
* ❌ Mixing calendar date with cohort logic

---

## 🧪 Validation Checklist

* Month 0 retention = 100%
* Retention never increases within same cohort
* Older cohorts decay smoothly
* New cohorts have fewer columns

If all true → model is healthy.

---

## 📌 When to Extend

You can safely add:

* Revenue retention
* Profit retention
* Cohort by branch / segment

Just extend the **customer-month fact**, not item-level sales.

